    {
      "id": "s2-hepatic-box",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 660, "y": 720,
      "width": 340, "height": 320,
      "angle": 0, "strokeColor": "#e67700", "backgroundColor": "#fff3bf",
      "fillStyle": "solid", "strokeWidth": 2, "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0, "opacity": 100,
      "roundness": { "type": 3 },
      "groupIds": [], "frameId": null, "index": "ac8"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2-hepatic-lbl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 668, "y": 726,
      "width": 324, "height": 308,
      "text": "Hepatic ALP ↑\n\nRUQ Ultrasound:\n→ Dilated ducts?\n  YES → Extrahepatic obstruction\n    • Choledocholithiasis\n    • Malignancy / stricture\n    → MRCP or ERCP\n  NO → Intrahepatic cholestasis\n\nIntrahepatic differential:\n• Congestive hepatopathy\n  (most common in HF-ICU)\n  Correlates with TR severity,\n  RAP, right-sided pressures\n• Drug-induced: amiodarone,\n  antibiotics, TPN\n• Infiltrative: amyloidosis,\n  sarcoidosis, hepatic mets\n• PBC (check AMA, ANA)\n• PSC (check pANCA)\n• Sepsis-assoc. cholestasis\n\nRx: Decongestion if cardiac;\n etiology-specific if not",
      "fontSize": 11, "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left", "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e", "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid", "angle": 0, "roughness": 0, "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [], "frameId": null, "index": "ac9"
    },

    {
      "id": "s2-nonhepatic-box",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 1020, "y": 720,
      "width": 220, "height": 200,
      "angle": 0, "strokeColor": "#495057", "backgroundColor": "#f8f9fa",
      "fillStyle": "solid", "strokeWidth": 1, "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0, "opacity": 100,
      "roundness": { "type": 3 },
      "groupIds": [], "frameId": null, "index": "ac10"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2-nonhepatic-lbl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1028, "y": 726,
      "width": 204, "height": 188,
      "text": "Non-Hepatic ALP ↑\n(GGT normal)\n\n• Bone: Paget's, mets,\n  healing fracture\n• Pregnancy\n• Growth (children)\n• Intestinal ALP\n  (post-prandial)\n\nWorkup:\n• ALP isoenzymes\n• Bone scan if mets\n  suspected",
      "fontSize": 11, "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left", "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e", "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid", "angle": 0, "roughness": 0, "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [], "frameId": null, "index": "ac11"
    },

    {
      "id": "s2-low-box",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 660, "y": 1060,
      "width": 580, "height": 120,
      "angle": 0, "strokeColor": "#495057", "backgroundColor": "#f8f9fa",
      "fillStyle": "solid", "strokeWidth": 1, "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 0, "opacity": 100,
      "roundness": { "type": 3 },
      "groupIds": [], "frameId": null, "index": "ac12"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2-low-lbl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 668, "y": 1066,
      "width": 564, "height": 108,
      "text": "LOW ALP (uncommon but clinically relevant):\n• Acute severe illness (most common ICU cause — epiphenomenon)\n• Malnutrition / caloric depletion\n• Zinc or magnesium deficiency\n• Hypothyroidism\n• Antiresorptive therapy (bisphosphonates, denosumab)\n• Hypophosphatasia (rare genetic; suspect if persistent low ALP +\n  musculoskeletal sx/fractures/dental anomalies → check PLP, phosphoethanolamine)\n• Wilson disease, pernicious anemia",
      "fontSize": 11, "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left", "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e", "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid", "angle": 0, "roughness": 0, "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [], "frameId": null, "index": "ac13"
    },

    {
      "id": "s2-prog-box",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 660, "y": 1200,
      "width": 580, "height": 70,
      "angle": 0, "strokeColor": "#e67700", "backgroundColor": "#fff9db",
      "fillStyle": "solid", "strokeWidth": 1, "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 0, "opacity": 100,
      "roundness": { "type": 3 },
      "groupIds": [], "frameId": null, "index": "ac14"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2-prog-lbl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 668, "y": 1208,
      "width": 564, "height": 56,
      "text": "PROGNOSIS: Isolated elevated ALP in ADHF (SURVIVE trial) correlated with systemic\ncongestion + elevated right-sided pressures. Predicted increased 180-day mortality\n(34.9% vs 23.5%, p=0.001) [Nikolaou 2013]",
      "fontSize": 11, "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left", "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e", "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid", "angle": 0, "roughness": 0, "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [], "frameId": null, "index": "ac15"
    },

    {
      "id": "sec3-hdr",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 1280, "y": 530,
      "width": 560, "height": 36,
      "angle": 0, "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e", "backgroundColor": "#2f9e44",
      "fillStyle": "solid", "strokeWidth": 2, "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0, "opacity": 100,
      "roundness": { "type": 3 },
      "groupIds": [], "frameId": null, "index": "ad0"
    },
    {
      "id": "sec3-hdr-lbl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1290, "y": 540,
      "width": 540, "height": 16,
      "text": "SECTION 3 — GAMMA-GLUTAMYL TRANSFERASE (GGT)",
      "fontSize": 14, "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "center", "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "strokeColor": "#ffffff", "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid", "angle": 0, "roughness": 0, "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [], "frameId": null, "index": "ad1"
    },

    {
      "id": "s3-box",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 1280, "y": 580,
      "width": 560, "height": 380,
      "angle": 0, "strokeColor": "#2f9e44", "backgroundColor": "#ebfbee",
      "fillStyle": "solid", "strokeWidth": 2, "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0, "opacity": 100,
      "roundness": { "type": 3 },
      "groupIds": [], "frameId": null, "index": "ad2"
    },
    {
      "id": "s3-lbl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1290, "y": 588,
      "width": 540, "height": 365,
      "text": "ELEVATED GGT\n\nPrimary utility: Confirms ALP elevation is hepatic\n(GGT is NOT present in bone — critical distinction)\n\nSTEP 1: HF-specific interpretation\n• GGT + ALP + TBili = cholestatic triad of congestive\n  hepatopathy\n• Correlates with JVD, peripheral edema, TR grade,\n  NYHA class, and right-sided filling pressures\n• GGT inversely associated with survival in stable HF\n\nSTEP 2: Non-cardiac causes to exclude\n• Alcohol use: GGT elevated in >50% of heavy drinkers\n  even without overt liver disease — most sensitive\n  marker of alcohol intake\n• Medications: phenytoin, barbiturates, carbamazepine\n• Pancreatic disease, DM, renal failure, emphysema\n• NAFLD / NASH\n• Biliary obstruction (same workup as elevated ALP)\n\nSTEP 3: Management\n• GGT alone should NOT be used as a standalone\n  screening test for liver disease\n• In HF-ICU: elevated GGT → assess right-sided\n  hemodynamics → decongestion\n\nNOTE: Low GGT is not a recognized clinical entity\nand does not require workup",
      "fontSize": 11, "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left", "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e", "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid", "angle": 0, "roughness": 0, "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [], "frameId": null, "index": "ad3"
    },

